1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,490
Eight girls at an Illinois high school, and every friendship between them.

2
00:00:04,700 --> 00:00:07,880
On average, each girl has two and a half friends.

3
00:00:08,066 --> 00:00:10,706
How many friends does the average friend have?

4
00:00:11,306 --> 00:00:12,166
Pick one.

5
00:00:14,266 --> 00:00:20,046
The answer tells flu researchers whom to watch: random students, or the friends they name.

6
00:00:20,733 --> 00:00:26,563
The natural guess is two and a half again: every friend is one of these same eight girls.

7
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:31,000
Check that on a rewired class where every girl has two friends.

8
00:00:31,300 --> 00:00:34,680
Send a token both ways along every friendship.

9
00:00:35,133 --> 00:00:38,873
Each token reads the friend count where it lands: always two.

10
00:00:39,373 --> 00:00:42,053
Both averages are two: the guess holds.

11
00:00:42,766 --> 00:00:46,616
Now restore the real friendships and send the tokens again.

12
00:00:46,916 --> 00:00:50,366
They read one, two, three, or four, and average three.

13
00:00:50,966 --> 00:00:59,516
The guess fell short by half a friend, because the tokens piled up unevenly: four on Sue, four on Alice, one on Betty.

14
00:01:00,233 --> 00:01:05,773
Line the girls up by friend count, two at each count: they balance at two and a half.

15
00:01:06,366 --> 00:01:11,366
The tokens stack higher on the right, one per friend, so they balance at three.

16
00:01:12,033 --> 00:01:15,733
Sue holds four tokens, each worth four: sixteen.

17
00:01:16,233 --> 00:01:20,753
A girl with k friends adds k squared on top, and k on the bottom.

18
00:01:21,200 --> 00:01:30,260
Now divide top and bottom by the eight girls: the average of k squared, seven and a half, over the average count, two and a half.

19
00:01:30,866 --> 00:01:33,386
Where does the extra half come from?

20
00:01:34,033 --> 00:01:39,353
Measure each girl's distance from the girls' balance point, two and a half, and square it.

21
00:01:40,853 --> 00:01:45,163
The squares average one and a quarter, the variance, sigma squared.

22
00:01:46,200 --> 00:01:47,840
Split Sue's count, four,

23
00:01:48,240 --> 00:01:50,550
into the balance point, two and a half,

24
00:01:50,950 --> 00:01:53,090
plus her distance, one and a half.

25
00:01:53,590 --> 00:01:58,280
Squaring gives two and a half squared, her distance squared, and a cross term.

26
00:01:58,880 --> 00:02:00,700
Every girl splits this way.

27
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:06,710
Because the beam balances, the distances add to zero, and the cross terms cancel.

28
00:02:07,766 --> 00:02:11,706
So the average of k squared is mu squared plus sigma squared.

29
00:02:13,306 --> 00:02:16,826
And friends average mu, plus sigma squared over mu.

30
00:02:18,026 --> 00:02:19,546
Here that's two and a half,

31
00:02:19,846 --> 00:02:23,476
plus a half: exactly the extra half friend we measured.

32
00:02:24,376 --> 00:02:26,826
With no spread, the ring had no gap.

33
00:02:28,833 --> 00:02:35,873
In twenty eleven, Facebook users averaged about a hundred ninety friends, and a few had thousands.

34
00:02:36,273 --> 00:02:40,473
If every friendship sent tokens, what would the tokens average?

35
00:02:42,533 --> 00:02:47,453
About six hundred thirty five: the spread term is more than twice the mean.

36
00:02:48,353 --> 00:02:53,523
And ninety three percent of users had fewer friends than their friends averaged.

37
00:02:53,823 --> 00:02:55,733
Odds are, that includes you.

38
00:02:56,433 --> 00:02:59,593
Christakis and Fowler put this to work at Harvard.

39
00:02:59,893 --> 00:03:06,143
Named friends have more friends, so they sit closer to everyone else, and should catch flu first.

40
00:03:06,643 --> 00:03:12,063
They tracked flu diagnoses in random students, and in the friends the students named.

41
00:03:12,363 --> 00:03:17,843
The friends' curve rose first: fitted curves put them thirteen point nine days ahead.

42
00:03:18,743 --> 00:03:22,553
With self-reported symptoms, the lead shrank to about three.

43
00:03:24,366 --> 00:03:27,586
Betty looks at her one friend, Sue, and sees four.

44
00:03:28,386 --> 00:03:29,976
Most of us are Betty.

